<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sharjah Fort</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1820">1820</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Al Hisn Sharjah, Al Hisn Fort</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sharjah Fort, known as Al Hisn, is a fort in the centre of Sharjah, United Arab <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirates</a>, built around 1820 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-bin-saqr-al-qasimi">Sultan bin Saqr Al Qasimi</a>. For about 150 years it was the residence of the ruling family and the seat of government. Most of it was pulled down in 1969, but it was rebuilt in the 1990s on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-bin-muhammad-al-qasimi">Sultan bin Muhammad Al Qasimi</a> and is now a museum.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sharjah-fort.jpg" alt="Sharjah Fort" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alexandermcnabb · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sharjah_Fort_(Al_Hisn).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The fort is a two-storey building of rock, coral and adobe. Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-bin-saqr-al-qasimi">Sultan bin Saqr Al Qasimi</a> had it built after Sharjah acceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/general-maritime-treaty-of-1820">General Maritime Treaty of 1820</a> with the British. British records of 1830 describe it as standing a little inland and mounting six pieces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>, with some detached towers; in case of alarm it was stockaded with date trees and wood, enough to repel an attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> though of little use against regular troops.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalid-bin-muhammad-al-qasimi">Khalid bin Muhammad Al Qasimi</a> ordered the fort's demolition because he wished to remove all trace of the previous ruler, Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saqr-bin-sultan-al-qasimi">Saqr bin Sultan Al Qasimi</a>. His brother, Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-bin-muhammad-al-qasimi">Sultan bin Muhammad Al Qasimi</a>, then studying in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, hurried home to stop it. He arrived too late to save most of the building but persuaded his brother to halt the work. Only one tower survived, called Kubs, which became known locally as the Burj, Arabic for tower, and gave its name to the square in which it stands. Saqr bin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a>, whose memory the demolition had been meant to erase, returned to Sharjah in 1972 in a failed coup attempt in which Sheikh Khalid was killed.</p><p>Sheikh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> had noted the line of the remaining foundations and saved fittings from the site. Once he had become Ruler of Sharjah, the fort was rebuilt between January 1996 and April 1997 with its original doors and windows, as part of the programme known as the Heart of Sharjah, which restores the traditional core of the old port city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sharjah Fort” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Defence rested on the ruler’s retainers, armed townsmen and allied Bedouin, supported by watchtowers and forts such as the <b>Al Hisn</b> fort in Sharjah town.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sheikhdom-of-sharjah">Sheikhdom of Sharjah · Military</a></li></ul></section>
